body:
No existing test — unit or golden — seems to exercise a literal of the rule's own target type nested inside another matched literal's `wrap`-targeted field. `lit-rule-only`'s "Nested" fixture comes closest, but it nests the matched literal inside a plain, non-matched `holder` struct field rather than inside a wrap template's splice point, so it never reaches the `dst.Clone`-orphaning path.

That gap is exactly what let the nested-literal bug ship undetected (see the `apply_lit.go` comment above). Worth adding a regression test alongside the fix, close to the existing wrap tests in this file:

```go
func TestApplyLitRule_WrapPreservesNestedMatchedLiteral(t *testing.T) {
	inner := transportLit(keyed("MaxIdleConns", "100"))
	outer := transportLit(&dst.KeyValueExpr{
		Key:   &dst.Ident{Name: "Proxy"},
		Value: &dst.CallExpr{Fun: &dst.Ident{Name: "identity"}, Args: []dst.Expr{inner}},
	})
	file := makeLitFile(outer)
	r := transportRule(
		&rule.InstLitField{Name: "Proxy", Wrap: "wrap({{ . }})"},
		&rule.InstLitField{Name: "Internal", Value: "true"},
	)

	ip := newTestPhase()
	require.NoError(t, ip.applyLitRule(context.Background(), r, file))

	// Both the outer and the nested literal are of the rule's target type;
	// both must end up instrumented.
}
```

A golden fixture pairing an `io.LimitedReader` nested inside another `io.LimitedReader`'s wrapped `R` field (a realistic reader-chaining pattern) would catch this at the integration level too.

