body:
Neither the `req.URL != nil` guard nor the `req.Host` fallback can trigger on the middleware path: `classifyOperation(req.URL.Path)` (line 165) and `getProviderName(req.URL.Host)` (line 171) both dereference `req.URL` unconditionally before this helper runs, so a nil `req.URL` would already have panicked upstream.

That is fine for a standalone helper, but as written it reads as though `req.Host` is a real production source of the address, and two of the eight test cases assert behavior the middleware can never reach. A short comment makes the intent explicit:

```suggestion
	// req.URL is always non-nil on the middleware path (classifyOperation and
	// getProviderName dereference it first). The nil guard and req.Host fallback
	// below keep this helper safe for direct callers and unit tests.
	if req.URL != nil {
		host = req.URL.Hostname()
		portStr = req.URL.Port()
	}
	if host == "" && req.Host != "" {
		if h, p, err := net.SplitHostPort(req.Host); err == nil {
			host = h
			portStr = p
		} else {
			host = req.Host
		}
	}
